per is kept in authored/evidence-status.json.","citation_ids":["cite.long.thm-second-channel"],"evidence_ids":["mapping.long.thm-second-channel"],"id":"long.thm-second-channel","kind":"claim","label":"Long record Theorem 6.4 (Second-channel phase separation sufficiency)","locator":"Long record Theorem 6.4, p. 46, label thm:second-channel","source_id":"paper.long.pdf","statement":"*Define $P_n=4^n\\bigl(2r_n(1/2)-2^{-n}\\bigr)$. If\n$$\n\\frac16+\\frac{37}{56}\\,2^{-n}\\le\n \\left|P_n-\\frac13\\right|\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge1,\n$$\nthen $1/2\\in\\mathcal A$. These are exact rational inequalities because the target and every finite greedy remainder are rational. The cited finite calculation establishes them for $1\\le n\\le6$; it therefore suffices to prove them for every $n\\ge7$.*\n\n*Unlike Theorem 6.3, this hypothesis concerns every positive rank, not only skipped ranks. It excludes an explicit interval around $1/3$. For example, $P_n\\notin(0,1)$ implies the displayed inequality when $n\\ge2$, but no such avoidance theorem for the whole orbit is proved here.*","status":"unverified"},"ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-second-channel","relations":[{"derived":false,"family":"arguments","from":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/long.thm-second-channel","id":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-second-channel%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.half_mem_mersenneAchievementSet_of_secondChannelSeparation%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","kind":"formalizes","record_ref":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/edges/%5B%22long.thm-second-channel%22%2C%22lean.Erdos249257.half_mem_mersenneAchievementSet_of_secondChannelSeparation%22%2C%22formalizes%22%5D","semantic_class":"formalization_correspondence","to":"sha256:f31aeb6174afd909393ada9bc329cd29dc9b1b6a0de8f72a49f18ab372947af5/nodes/lean.Erdos